{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/882921-long-term-viability-of-electronic-documents","id":882921,"title":"Long term viability of electronic documents","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-05-05T09:28:30-07:00","id":882921,"title":"Long term viability of electronic documents","content":"This is a general question, and I didn't know where to put it. How does White Wolf/Onyx Path plan to approach the short lifespan of electronic documents? I'm not really asking for a specific business plan or anything, obviously that wouldn't be forthcoming. However, since electronic files don't last anywhere near as long as printed files, is this a concern for how to handle product releases?<br /> <br /> I wonder about this as the gaming industry starts to really promote electronic documents as the way forward. Just curious.<br /> <br /> Thank you","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9456-phil_ozzyfer","name":"Phil_OzzyFer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-05T22:05:06-07:00","id":883486,"title":"","content":"If my file gets corrupt, I download a new copy from drive-thru. <br /> <br /> I still have data archives from 25 years ago. The same can't be said of many of my papers.<br /> <br /> So I'm kinda double-confused here, one in the assumption that digital has lower lifespan than physical, and second that there's any particular issue with digital.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-05T22:34:04-07:00","id":883498,"title":"","content":"There are issues with the lack of permanence of digital files... but that's not really a everyday person problem. That's a large scale institutional problem over decades.<br /> <br /> Historians are worried about this sort of thing (esp. as old files are not archived in accessible formats) because it is very possible that we're going to find ourselves with massive holes in the historical record on account of poor digital archiving and/or any sort of EMP bursts over certain storage facility heavy areas and/or purposeful digital attacks. There's too much data, in too many formats, to hope to maintain it all in the current fashion we do on a societal level.<br /> <br /> But, like I said, this is meaningless for something like RPG ebooks. Such products are essentially (if in a very niche fashion) high priority for being maintained because they're not things like record archives meant for storage. They're products actively being sold.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-06T09:31:32-07:00","id":883846,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/882921-long-term-viability-of-electronic-documents?p=883486#post883486\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If my file gets corrupt, I download a new copy from drive-thru.<br /> <br /> I still have data archives from 25 years ago. The same can't be said of many of my papers.<br /> <br /> So I'm kinda double-confused here, one in the assumption that digital has lower lifespan than physical, and second that there's any particular issue with digital.</div> </div> </div> </div>Books last 100+ years. How long does the technology last that makes file formats accessible? <br /> <br /> Heavy Arms hit the nail on the head explaining how this matters for digital files. <br /> <br /> &quot;But, like I said, this is meaningless for something like RPG ebooks. Such products are essentially (if in a very niche fashion) high priority for being maintained because they're not things like record archives meant for storage. They're products actively being sold.&quot;<br /> <br /> This above quote, by Heavy Arms is a great point, and I hadn't considered that when I put my question up. However, I guess where my question was leading is, what can the RPG industry do to ensure that the problems around the longevity of digital files never really becomes an issue?<br /> <br /> I would argue that this is an issue that factors into people's lives on an everyday basis. Consider the evolution of technology just from when White Wolf began publishing, how much money has been spent on tech to keep digital files current, versus one hardcover book? Consider that in 2008 HD DVD became obsolete just because the movie studios decided to back Blu-Ray. It's not a direct comparison, but I think it's useful to keep these things in mind. From one day to the next a DVD collection essentially became junk. It's true that because these products (I'm talking about games here, not DVDs) are for sale it helps keep them current, but at the same time, that currency is dependent on factors beyond sales.<br /> <br /> I have the impression that digital publishing is in a good position to lead the way for device agnostic file formats that could alleviate a lot of the problems digital files cause with regards to their overall permanence. I have no idea what that would look like, but it would definitely be advantageous for RPGs to be involved.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9456-phil_ozzyfer","name":"Phil_OzzyFer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-06T14:09:36-07:00","id":884059,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Phil_OzzyFer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/882921-long-term-viability-of-electronic-documents?p=883846#post883846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, I guess where my question was leading is, what can the RPG industry do to ensure that the problems around the longevity of digital files never really becomes an issue?</div> </div> </div> </div>What' they're already doing?<br /> <br /> At this point, RPG products are in as good as position as possible to avoid any issues with being lost into the digital record keeping whole. They exist in multiple device agnostic forms (Onyx Path as copies of their books in text-only manuscript formats and multiple forms of the final documents), and print form (since Rich gets test copies of the print books to make sure they're correct).<br /> <br /> There's really not much more that can be done.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I would argue that this is an issue that factors into people's lives on an everyday basis. Consider the evolution of technology just from when White Wolf began publishing, how much money has been spent on tech to keep digital files current, versus one hardcover book? </div> </div> </div>This seems a bit blind to the reality of publishing in the 90s. White Wolf was virtually always working on books in a digital format because by that point that was the primary method of layout and printing. While hard copy formats were part of record keeping as well, failures to keep digital files current has actually cost them money because turning a hardcopy into a new digital file is extremely time intensive/expensive compared to maintaining the digital files.<br /> <br /> Keeping digital files current is far less expensive than turning a hardcopy into a new digital copy years later; <b>if</b> there's a continual demand for the product.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> From one day to the next a DVD collection essentially became junk. </div> </div> </div>There's a difference between them losing monetary value, being more expensive to maintain, and being outright junk. I have a rather extensive VHS and DVD collection - along with other fun things lik 3.5 floppies - they all still work just fine. For the ones where I have access to higher quality videos via things like streaming services... yeah, they're kind of useless, but plenty of them aren't available like that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I have the impression that digital publishing is in a good position to lead the way for device agnostic file formats that could alleviate a lot of the problems digital files cause with regards to their overall permanence. I have no idea what that would look like, but it would definitely be advantageous for RPGs to be involved. </div> </div> </div>The majority of digital RPG products are released in as agnostic formats as possible (keeping in mind that there's no universal format right now because device manufactures don't want there to be).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-08T04:23:13-07:00","id":885287,"title":"","content":"The other thing to remember is a DVD collection isn't junk. I can't think of any Blu-ray player that can't run DVDs.That's because it's useful to have backwards compatibility with things that aren't software designed to run on a specific sort of machine/OS. It's something that people want and because current media basically takes the same physical form for size, shape such, why not? If they didn't, people would look at the machine for sale next to it that doesn't require them to buy a whole new media collection.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-08T07:36:32-07:00","id":885368,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/882921-long-term-viability-of-electronic-documents?p=885287#post885287\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The other thing to remember is a DVD collection isn't junk. I can't think of any Blu-ray player that can't run DVDs.That's because it's useful to have backwards compatibility with things that aren't software designed to run on a specific sort of machine/OS. It's something that people want and because current media basically takes the same physical form for size, shape such, why not? If they didn't, people would look at the machine for sale next to it that doesn't require them to buy a whole new media collection.</div> </div> </div> </div>True, but the next generation of players won't take DVDs and may not even match the form factor for Blu-Rays.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-08T08:10:17-07:00","id":885386,"title":"","content":"Ummm, which generation do you mean? Because best as I can see, future technologies are still meant to be backward compatible, including using the same form.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-08T11:36:06-07:00","id":885524,"title":"","content":"Hardware wise maybe. BUT software wise is another issue. I am Using Win 8.1 and already have some of my older games (mostly from the Win 95/98 era) that won't even get recognized to load.. even if i put it into '98 compatibility mode).. Same with some of the newer games (2000 era) and Win 10..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-08T13:40:25-07:00","id":885654,"title":"","content":"The controls on software backwards compatibility are ultimately the product of short-sighted corporate interests.<br /> <br /> The need to maintain some backwards compatibility as a sign of good faith, and there is a cost for them to do so. So eventually they get to say, &quot;maintaining this much backwards compatibility on our end is too costly so we're going to sop.&quot; And of course, that lets them sell you the same product again in a new format (for them, preferably of the &quot;renting it&quot; model that they're clogging the system with).<br /> <br /> Again though, this is very much beyond the scope of RPG products. D&amp;D tried subscription RPG access... and thankfully it didn't work out very well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-08T17:32:50-07:00","id":885822,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/882921-long-term-viability-of-electronic-documents?p=885524#post885524\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hardware wise maybe. BUT software wise is another issue. I am Using Win 8.1 and already have some of my older games (mostly from the Win 95/98 era) that won't even get recognized to load.. even if i put it into '98 compatibility mode).. Same with some of the newer games (2000 era) and Win 10..</div> </div> </div> </div>table top games are not software programs. There is much better support/reason to support old data file formats and/or converting them then there is old software or the capacity to convert old software.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-09T07:47:35-07:00","id":886240,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/882921-long-term-viability-of-electronic-documents?p=885386#post885386\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ummm, which generation do you mean? Because best as I can see, future technologies are still meant to be backward compatible, including using the same form.</div> </div> </div> </div>Really? My DVD player won't play VHS tapes. They're not even the right shape.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-09T08:09:37-07:00","id":886272,"title":"","content":"I'm talking about future technologies. All the ones that I could find discussed seem backwards compatible with Blu-ray and DVD. Unless there's a very sudden change in the technologies for storage, there's no comparison there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-09T14:47:09-07:00","id":886643,"title":"","content":"The DVD example was just meant to be a way to show how people's lives can be impacted on a day to day basis as far as storing electronic documents are concerned. Earlier it was stated that long term storage issues only matter to institutions, or on a large scale, and that isn't actually always the case. I think when I posted this initially, one of things I took for granted was the extent to which people project forward what was or was not going to change. Really, all the gaming industry can do is deal with it's current set of circumstances, and file formats like .pdf are pretty versatile. I do still think it's in the publishing industry's interest to get together and discuss ways of dealing with long-term storage/access. Getting rid of planned obsolescence would help out as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9456-phil_ozzyfer","name":"Phil_OzzyFer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-09T14:55:22-07:00","id":886650,"title":"","content":"The thing is that the record/tape/floppy/DVD series of storage, is that it while it did lead to costs to the consumers, we didn't really lose anything. The problem with sustainable digital storage isn't that storage formats change. The entertainment industry has a vested interest in transferring product onto new storage formats. If they don't, they risk losing rights over old products. Every time that an album,movie, game, etc. gets updated, it helps them extend their copyright protections instead of those things going public domain.<br /> <br /> Institutional systems don't have the same pressure.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-09T15:01:21-07:00","id":886658,"title":"","content":"I'm surprised the topic is dwelling so much on file integrity.<br /> <br /> Usually these complaints focus on the merchant, in this case DriveThruRPG. If they go down, you can't replace your old books.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-09T15:46:58-07:00","id":886689,"title":"","content":"I have all my OBS products backed up in a digital format I can print on my own if I need to. My PoD books are nicer for when I want a physical copy though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-11T13:48:18-07:00","id":888528,"title":"","content":"(This thread is drifting a bit from the original topic, but...) Copyright isn't necessarily based on file formats for protection, although the specifics of the laws depend on countries. Every year new stuff enters the public domain. It's in the interest of tech producers to develop new formats because they make money by forcing everyone to re-adapt. Granted, I'm not a legal expert, but purchasing material that is protected by copyright grants you access to the content, the medium is irrelevant.Think about music, if you bought a 12&quot; LP in the seventies of something like, say, 'Dark Side of the Moon' by Pink Floyd, this has been re-released on cassette, CD, DVD, MP3, and this will continue. You may have purchased that album on all of those formats so you could keep listening to it, but its the exact same album. In my mind, and this is partially where my argument towards the publishing industry comes back to, is that there has to be some way of creating a means that makes material more accessible. If all you're paying for is the legal right to enjoy the music, why should it be necessary to re-purchase the album you've already paid to use/access? The means of access isn't the only issue though. Even if Drivethrurpg stays up forever, digital file formats decay, differently than physical ones, but over time the files become corrupted. Long-term access to digital documents is a massive complicated issue, as the responses here all indicate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9456-phil_ozzyfer","name":"Phil_OzzyFer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-11T15:18:06-07:00","id":888607,"title":"","content":"Copyright isn't base on file formats for protection. However in many countries (esp. ones with powerful media industry lobbies, like the US), new formats allow a public domain loophole. They can re-release the same product in a new format, call it a &quot;new&quot; product, and the time until the copyright runs out gets essentially reset (though this requires a certain amount of legal heavy handed tactics that are... questionable but IP laws are a weird).<br /> <br /> If I personally buy a LP of something, I have the right to that copy, and the right to make archival/storage copies. Which means I can personally update it to new formats (and if I have the equipment/resources remaster it to maintain it's integrity long term). However, I can't make copies for other people.<br /> <br /> This is why there's been a movement towards treating digital content as something you're technically &quot;renting&quot; rather than something you own. If you only paid for access, instead of single copy ownership, you never owned it and never had the right to copy it; even for yourself. EULA are convoluted messes that hide this rather well. The (barely counts as an) upside however, is that it does make it far easier for content providers to maintain their files.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-11T15:35:30-07:00","id":888619,"title":"","content":"What Heavy Arms is a lot like how drug companies will release &quot;combos&quot; of two medications, because it lets them keep the patent because putting two different medications in one pill totally makes it new and so generic forms can't be released.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}